Z||000001||theogre||04-14-99||06:04 PM||||anyone had the roof off at all? even for a sunroof addon?<p>I think there are framing members above the door window. These would need cutting. the car would need other structural changes if that is the case. (((I'm not pulling my headliner to check, sorry.))) Putting in T tops usually causes the same body flex problems as a rag top.
Z||000002||Motorhead||04-15-99||02:14 AM||||Your'e right Ogre, I just had my headliner out for recovering and there are structural members above the door openings. Also, there is no structural support in the center of the top. T-Top equipped car would have to have a differently configured subframe. 
Z||000003||Pretender||04-15-99||03:14 AM||||i figured that it would be a little different, how much work can be expected for the swap?..if i took off the front fenders(taking a guess), out the headliner and unbolted the whole windshield w/ the roof(would this come out in one piece?)..i can't see the engine twisting the chassie enough to crack the glass.......:o)
Z||000004||theogre||04-15-99||08:01 AM||||the flex problem tends to be front to back. The cars own mass works against it. T-Top, and convertibles, use frame reinforcement under the car to make up for the lost overhead support.<p>the frame braces are installed in diferant ways. they can be weld/bolt up items, or could actually be inside the frame or rocker pannels. It depends on the car, and who did the conversion. Neither option is assembly line installed. (Only U.S. car built on the line as a convertable is/was Chrysler Le Baron as far as I know.)
